  <!-- ============================ COVER ============================ -->
  <header class="cover">
    <span class="eyebrow">Engineering case study &middot; AI legal intelligence</span>
    <h1>Self-Updating AI<br>Legal Knowledge Base</h1>
    <p class="lede">A knowledge base of US deposit law that keeps itself current — three Claude agents that research, then adversarially fact-check each other, then write strict JSON. Nothing reaches the library until a person accepts it, which is the whole product.</p>

    <div class="cover-meta">
      <div><span class="k">Role</span><span class="v">Sole developer</span></div>
      <div><span class="k">Timeline</span><span class="v">Jun &ndash; Aug 2026, 9 commits</span></div>
      <div><span class="k">Scope</span><span class="v">51 US jurisdictions, 106 seeded articles</span></div>
      <div><span class="k">Status</span><span class="v">Running; Render deploy configured</span></div>
    </div>

    <figure>
      <img src="/case-studies/img/depositlex/dashboard-pending.webp" alt="Reviewer dashboard showing 106 articles, 52 jurisdictions, 3 proposals pending review, and a Proposed updates panel listing three AI proposals with change type, confidence, jurisdiction and accept / review / decline actions.">
      <figcaption><span class="fignum">Fig. 1</span>The reviewer's console. Four counters, a search box scoped to one jurisdiction, and an inbox of proposals — each carrying its change type, the model's own confidence, and the three actions that decide it. <span class="fixture">The proposals shown throughout are hand-built fixtures, not model output</span>; see the note at the end of section 05.</figcaption>
    </figure>
